WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
Scenario: You are not signing in, but your phone

2
00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.667
displays an approval request for your work account. Safest

3
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:10.000
response: Option B: Deny the request and report it

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.333
to the helpdesk or security team. A prompt you

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.667
did not initiate may indicate someone has your password

6
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.000
or is attempting authentication. Option A is unsafe because

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.333
Repeated prompts can be an authentication-fatigue tactic; approving a

8
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.667
later one is unsafe. Option C is unsafe because

9
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.000
Public sharing may expose account identifiers or organizational information

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.333
and does not report the incident appropriately. Option D

11
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.037
is unsafe because Approval may authorize an attacker’s login attempt.

12
00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:40.370
In practice: Deny the request, do not disclose any

13
00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.704
code, and report unexpected or repeated prompts through the

14
00:00:43.704 --> 00:00:47.037
organization’s support channel. Key takeaway: Only approve authentication you

15
00:00:47.037 --> 00:00:50.000
initiated and can confidently match to your sign-in.
